<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 4</h2>
<p style="text-align: center;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Colors in Our World</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's an Orange Cat.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write numbers up to 10 with guidance.<br>Interactive: Write one-word answers to simple questions.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write numbers up to ten with teacher guidance to represent objects in drawings (e.g., drawing four flowers and writing "4").<br>Can respond to simple questions about objects or colors by writing one-word answers with teacher support (e.g., "What color is this?" Write "blue.").<br>Can use simple drawings to communicate about objects and add one-word descriptions (e.g., drawing a green leaf and writing "leaf").</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h3 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of familiar objects and colors (e.g., an orange cat, a blue bird, three green leaves). Students chorally identify them: "Orange cat!", "Blue bird!", "Three green leaves!". The teacher then distributes worksheets with outline drawings of these objects and spaces for numbers and color words. Students practice tracing the words for colors (e.g., "blue," "red," "green") and numbers (e.g., "1," "2," "3") associated with the drawings.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of "two red flowers." The teacher says "Two red flowers." and then writes "2" and "red" clearly on the board next to a drawing of two red flowers, verbalizing each letter and number as they write. The teacher also models the pronunciation of /l/ in "blue" and "leaf" as they write the words. They point to the "f" in "flower" and say /f/ to reinforce phonemic awareness.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'blue' a color or a number?" (Expected answer: "A color!")</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you write the number for two flowers?" (Teacher gestures to a drawing of two flowers and points to the number '2' on the board).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What sound does 'flower' start with? /f/ or /b/?" (Expected answer: "/f/").</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Colors in Our World".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, colorful chart titled "Colors in Our World" with simple illustrations of objects and corresponding written sentences and numbers. For example:
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Drawing of an orange cat) "It is an orange cat. 1 orange cat."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Drawing of two blue birds flying) "They are blue birds. 2 blue birds."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Drawing of three green leaves) "These are green leaves. 3 green leaves."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Drawing of a red flower) "This is a red flower. 1 red flower."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Drawing of four tall trees) "Many tall trees. 4 tall trees."</li>
          </ul>
        The teacher points to each illustration and reads the sentences clearly, emphasizing the color + noun structure and numbers, while students follow along. Students are encouraged to point to the words and numbers as the teacher reads.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Recognizing colors and objects: Identify familiar objects by color (e.g., "blue bird" or "red flower.") Describing with colors: Use simple color phrases in sentences (e.g., "That is a red bird.")</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "What color is the cat?" Students point to "orange" on the chart.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "How many blue birds?" Students point to "2" on the chart.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False: The teacher says, "It is a purple flower." (pointing to the red flower drawing). Students show thumbs up for True or thumbs down for False.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing numbers up to 10. Writing one-word answers for colors and objects. Grammar: Color + noun, WH-questions, Present simple. Vocabulary: bird, flower, cat, tree, house, leaf, sky, blue, purple, orange, green, red, two, three, four.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Color & Count Tracing"</b>
            Students receive a worksheet with various outline drawings (e.g., 1 cat, 3 flowers, 2 birds, 4 leaves). Next to each drawing, there is a dashed-line word for its color and a dashed-line number. For example: (Drawing of an orange cat) "______ orange cat. _____ 1 cat." (Drawing of 3 green leaves) "______ green leaves. _____ 3 leaves." Students trace the color word and the number, reinforcing correct spelling and numeral formation. The teacher circulates, offering individual support and checking tracing accuracy.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice: "Fill in the Blanks & Draw"</b>
            Students receive another worksheet with simple sentences and missing words or numbers, alongside space for a small drawing. For example: "It is a ______ bird." (picture of a blue bird). Students write "blue." "_______ red flowers." (picture of 4 red flowers). Students write "4." "What color is the house?" (picture of a red house). Students write "red." Students then draw a simple object and write its color and count next to it, e.g., draw one red flower and write "red" and "1."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction/Peer Check: "Picture & Word Match"</b>
            Students work in pairs. Each student receives a set of small picture cards (e.g., a green tree, a purple flower, a blue sky) and small word cards (e.g., "green," "purple," "blue," "tree," "flower," "sky," "1," "2"). Student A draws a picture on their mini-whiteboard (or paper), e.g., "two red flowers." Student B writes the number "2" and the word "red" next to the drawing. Then they switch roles. They check each other's written answers against the picture. The teacher encourages students to say "Good writing!" or "Try again!" (with gentle correction) to their partner.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Colors in Our World".</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "My Colorful World Booklet"</b>
          Students create a simple, small booklet titled "My Colorful World." Each page features a blank space for a drawing at the top and lines at the bottom for writing. Students are instructed to draw different objects from the scenario "Colors in Our World" (e.g., bird, flower, cat, tree, house, leaf, sky) and use their crayons to color them. For each drawing, they must write a simple description using the target grammar and vocabulary. The task emphasizes writing numbers up to 10 and one-word answers for colors/objects. The teacher provides examples on the board:
          <ul>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Drawing of an orange cat) "Orange cat." (or "1 orange cat.")</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Drawing of two blue birds) "2 blue birds."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Drawing of a green leaf) "Green leaf."</li>
          </ul>
        Students work individually to draw and write, focusing on clear drawings and legible writing. The teacher circulates, offering prompts like "What color is that?" or "How many leaves can you see?" to encourage written responses, and assisting with spelling when necessary.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and student work analysis.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their "My Colorful World Booklet." The teacher reviews each booklet for written accuracy and completion of the task.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Legible writing of numbers up to 10 to represent objects (e.g., "4 flowers").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct spelling of at least 3-4 target color words (e.g., "blue," "red," "green") as one-word answers or descriptions.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Drawings clearly communicate objects and colors.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to add simple one-word descriptions (e.g., "cat," "leaf") to drawings.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write the color ______." (Students write one color word they are confident with).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write the number ______." (Students write one number they are confident with, e.g., "3" or "5").</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to write today?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What color or number do I want to practice writing more?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson? (e.g., tracing activities, visual support, pair work).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students struggling with letter formation or spelling? (e.g., more direct instruction on letter strokes, word banks, peer modeling).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Was the pace appropriate for 1st graders? Should I have dedicated more time to individual feedback during production?</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Mediation & Integration), students will use their knowledge of colors, objects, and numbers to summarize information from their booklets and perhaps share them with another class or family members. This writing practice will be foundational for consolidating information in a simple communicative context.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>